1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,829
This is MATH 239, video 9. The topic is the Sum Lemma, the Product Lemma, and the String Lemma.

2
00:00:08,279 --> 00:00:19,543
After this video, you can do the five things in this list. The last one is Assignment 2 questions 2 and 3, and practice problems 1 to 3 of Assignment 3.

3
00:00:19,993 --> 00:00:31,961
A short recap of the last video. A weight function w gives each element of a set S a natural number. For each n, only finitely many elements have weight n.

4
00:00:32,411 --> 00:00:37,787
Here S is the set of subsets of 1, 2. The weight of a subset is its size.

5
00:00:38,237 --> 00:00:43,805
The generating series phi of S is the sum of x to the weight, over all elements.

6
00:00:44,255 --> 00:00:50,890
Group the elements by weight. One subset has weight 0, two have weight 1, and one has weight 2.

7
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:55,031
So phi of S is 1 plus 2 x plus x squared.

8
00:00:55,481 --> 00:01:08,174
Proposition 2.7 says: the coefficient of x to the n is the number of elements of weight n. For example, the coefficient of x is 2, and two subsets have weight 1.

9
00:01:08,624 --> 00:01:19,867
Most sets in this course are too large to list. But we can build them from small sets with three operations. The first is a union of two sets with no common element.

10
00:01:20,317 --> 00:01:22,834
The Sum Lemma says: the series add.

11
00:01:23,284 --> 00:01:28,127
The second operation is the Cartesian product: all pairs alpha, beta.

12
00:01:28,577 --> 00:01:31,819
The Product Lemma says: the series multiply.

13
00:01:32,269 --> 00:01:36,088
The third operation makes strings of elements of any length.

14
00:01:36,538 --> 00:01:41,274
The String Lemma says: the series is 1 over 1 minus phi of A.

15
00:01:41,724 --> 00:01:51,409
So each lemma turns a set operation into an operation on power series. We get the series of a large set from the series of small sets.

16
00:01:51,859 --> 00:01:59,945
Here is the Sum Lemma. A and B are disjoint: they have no common element. w is a weight function on the union.

17
00:02:00,395 --> 00:02:06,859
The two boxes show the elements of A and B. The gray label under each element is its weight.

18
00:02:07,309 --> 00:02:17,506
Count the elements of A by weight. One has weight 0, two have weight 1, none has weight 2, and one has weight 3. This is the row of phi of A.

19
00:02:17,956 --> 00:02:22,159
The same for B. One element of weight 1, and two of weight 2.

20
00:02:22,609 --> 00:02:31,910
Each element of the union is in exactly one of the two sets. So the number of elements of weight n in the union is the sum of the two counts.

21
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:35,411
For weight 1, that is 2 plus 1, which is 3.

22
00:02:35,861 --> 00:02:39,509
So phi of the union is phi of A plus phi of B.

23
00:02:39,959 --> 00:02:48,257
The weight on A and on B is the same function w. The A2 solutions write the disjoint union with a square cup.

24
00:02:48,707 --> 00:02:56,430
The proof has three lines. Line 1 is the definition of phi of the union: a sum over all its elements.

25
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:01,253
Line 2 splits the sum into a sum over A and a sum over B.

26
00:03:01,703 --> 00:03:10,834
Each element of the union is in A or in B, and the sets are disjoint. So each term occurs exactly once on the right side.

27
00:03:11,284 --> 00:03:16,788
Line 3 uses the definition again, for A and for B. This proves the lemma.

28
00:03:17,238 --> 00:03:25,686
The second line fails when the sets overlap. Here A is 1, 2 and B is 2, 3. The weight of an element is its value.

29
00:03:26,136 --> 00:03:28,248
The element 2 is in both sets.

30
00:03:28,698 --> 00:03:30,959
Phi of A is x plus x squared.

31
00:03:31,409 --> 00:03:34,140
Phi of B is x squared plus x cubed.

32
00:03:34,590 --> 00:03:39,219
Add them. The coefficient of x squared is 1 plus 1, which is 2.

33
00:03:39,669 --> 00:03:44,491
But the union is 1, 2, 3. It has only one element of weight 2.

34
00:03:44,941 --> 00:03:51,341
So the right side counts the common element twice. The second equality needs disjoint sets.

35
00:03:51,791 --> 00:04:00,303
An example. S is the set of all subsets of 1, 2, 3. The weight of a subset is its size. There are 8 subsets.

36
00:04:00,753 --> 00:04:05,617
Split S with one rule. A holds the subsets that do not contain 3.

37
00:04:06,067 --> 00:04:08,648
B holds the subsets that contain 3.

38
00:04:09,098 --> 00:04:13,151
No subset is in both, and every subset is in one of them.

39
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:22,924
A has one subset of size 0, two of size 1, and one of size 2. So phi of A is 1 plus 2 x plus x squared.

40
00:04:23,374 --> 00:04:29,262
Each subset in B is a subset of A with 3 added. So each weight goes up by 1.

41
00:04:29,712 --> 00:04:33,637
So phi of B is x plus 2 x squared plus x cubed.

42
00:04:34,087 --> 00:04:41,191
Add the rows. By the Sum Lemma, phi of S is 1 plus 3 x plus 3 x squared plus x cubed.

43
00:04:41,641 --> 00:04:46,377
For example, the coefficient of x squared is 1 plus 2, which is 3.

44
00:04:46,827 --> 00:04:50,539
The coefficients are 3 choose k, as in chapter 1.

45
00:04:50,989 --> 00:05:06,093
The Sum Lemma also works for infinitely many sets. Let A 0, A 1, A 2, and so on be pairwise disjoint. B is their union, with a weight function. Then phi of B is the sum of all phi of A j.

46
00:05:06,543 --> 00:05:13,754
Example: all binary strings, with weight equal to the length. S j is the set of strings of length j.

47
00:05:14,204 --> 00:05:20,241
S 0 holds only the empty string, epsilon. So phi of S 0 is 1.

48
00:05:20,691 --> 00:05:27,433
S 1 holds the strings 0 and 1. Both have weight 1, so phi of S 1 is 2 x.

49
00:05:27,883 --> 00:05:34,091
S 2 has 4 strings, all of weight 2. So its row has a single 4, at x squared.

50
00:05:34,541 --> 00:05:41,965
In general, S j has 2 to the j strings, all of weight j. Each row has one nonzero cell.

51
00:05:42,415 --> 00:05:47,897
Add the rows. Phi of S is the sum of 2 to the j times x to the j.

52
00:05:48,347 --> 00:05:52,571
This is the geometric series 1 over 1 minus 2 x.

53
00:05:53,021 --> 00:05:59,016
The proof of the lemma uses the same steps as the Sum Lemma. It is Exercise 2.6.

54
00:05:59,466 --> 00:06:03,733
Now Assignment 2, question 2 a. Pause the video and try it.

55
00:06:04,183 --> 00:06:11,820
The weight has one formula for even numbers, and another for odd numbers. The table shows the first ten weights.

56
00:06:12,270 --> 00:06:18,009
The two formulas suggest the split. Let E be the even numbers. Write a as 2 m.

57
00:06:18,459 --> 00:06:27,120
Then w equals 3 times 2 m, plus 1, which is 6 m plus 1. The weights are 1, 7, 13, and so on.

58
00:06:27,570 --> 00:06:31,410
Let O be the odd numbers. Write a as 2 m plus 1.

59
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:45,407
Then w equals 3 times 2 m plus 1, minus 1, over 2. That is 6 m plus 2, over 2, which is 3 m plus 1. The weights are 1, 4, 7, 10, and so on.

60
00:06:45,857 --> 00:06:53,238
Phi of E has a 1 at each weight of an even number: x, x to the 7, x to the 13, and so on.

61
00:06:53,688 --> 00:06:58,957
Phi of O has a 1 at x, x to the 4, x to the 7, and so on.

62
00:06:59,407 --> 00:07:08,175
E and O are disjoint, and their union is all of N. So by the Sum Lemma, phi of N is phi of E plus phi of O.

63
00:07:08,625 --> 00:07:13,489
Add the rows. The weights 1, 7 and 13 each have two elements.

64
00:07:13,939 --> 00:07:20,233
Each part is a geometric series. Phi of E is x over 1 minus x to the 6.

65
00:07:20,683 --> 00:07:23,733
Phi of O is x over 1 minus x cubed.

66
00:07:24,183 --> 00:07:39,842
Use the common denominator 1 minus x to the 6. It equals 1 minus x cubed, times 1 plus x cubed. So the second fraction becomes x times 1 plus x cubed, over 1 minus x to the 6.

67
00:07:40,292 --> 00:07:52,196
Add the tops: x plus x plus x to the 4 is 2 x plus x to the 4. So phi of N is 2 x plus x to the 4, over 1 minus x to the 6.

68
00:07:52,646 --> 00:07:58,811
Next, the Product Lemma. Here A has three elements. The gray labels give their weights, omega.

69
00:07:59,261 --> 00:08:01,843
B has two elements, with weights nu.

70
00:08:02,293 --> 00:08:10,037
The Cartesian product A times B is the set of all pairs alpha, beta. The grid has one cell for each pair.

71
00:08:10,487 --> 00:08:23,308
Define the weight of a pair as omega of alpha plus nu of beta. Each cell shows this sum. For example, the pair alpha 1, beta 1 has weight 0 plus 1, which is 1.

72
00:08:23,758 --> 00:08:28,473
Look at weight 2. Three cells have weight 2. They are marked in orange.

73
00:08:28,923 --> 00:08:32,464
Now the two series. Phi of A is 1 plus 2 x.

74
00:08:32,914 --> 00:08:35,239
Phi of B is x plus x squared.

75
00:08:35,689 --> 00:08:48,873
Multiply them. 1 plus 2 x, times x plus x squared, is x plus x squared plus 2 x squared plus 2 x cubed. That is x plus 3 x squared plus 2 x cubed.

76
00:08:49,323 --> 00:08:54,337
The coefficient of x squared is 3. This is the number of orange cells.

77
00:08:54,787 --> 00:09:01,464
The Product Lemma says that this always holds: phi of A times B is phi of A times phi of B.

78
00:09:01,914 --> 00:09:06,245
The proof has two parts. First, eta must be a weight function.

79
00:09:06,695 --> 00:09:15,207
Group the elements of A by weight: A 0, A 1, A 2. Here A 0 has 1 element, A 1 has 2, and A 2 has 1.

80
00:09:15,657 --> 00:09:21,566
Group B the same way: B 0 has 1 element, B 1 has 2, and B 2 has 2.

81
00:09:22,016 --> 00:09:29,739
Each block of the grid is a set A k times B l. For example, the orange block is A 1 times B 2.

82
00:09:30,189 --> 00:09:46,786
A pair has weight n exactly when its first part has some weight k, and its second part has weight n minus k. So the pairs of weight n are the union of the blocks A k times B n minus k. Here n is 2: the purple blocks.

83
00:09:47,236 --> 00:09:54,596
There are n plus 1 such blocks, and each block is finite. So only finitely many pairs have weight n.

84
00:09:55,046 --> 00:10:02,278
For n equal to 2, the count is 1 times 2, plus 2 times 2, plus 1 times 1. That is 7.

85
00:10:02,728 --> 00:10:13,587
Second, the series. Write phi of A times B as a double sum, over alpha in A and beta in B, of x to the omega of alpha plus nu of beta.

86
00:10:14,037 --> 00:10:21,375
x to a sum is a product of two powers. So the double sum factors into a product of two single sums.

87
00:10:21,825 --> 00:10:24,193
These are phi of A and phi of B.

88
00:10:24,643 --> 00:10:36,291
The coefficient of x to the n in the product is the sum of a k times b n minus k. This is the same count as the blocks: 7 for n equal to 2.

89
00:10:36,741 --> 00:10:45,531
Now part b. Pause the video and try it. The set is all pairs a, b of natural numbers, with weight 3 a plus 2 b.

90
00:10:45,981 --> 00:10:53,127
The key step: write the weight as a sum. w 1 of a is 3 a, and w 2 of b is 2 b.

91
00:10:53,577 --> 00:10:59,913
The grid shows the weight of each pair. Rows are values of a, and columns are values of b.

92
00:11:00,363 --> 00:11:06,742
For example, row 1, column 2 holds 3 times 1 plus 2 times 2, which is 7.

93
00:11:07,192 --> 00:11:14,936
Two pairs have weight 6: a equal to 2 with b equal to 0, and a equal to 0 with b equal to 3.

94
00:11:15,386 --> 00:11:23,045
Each factor is a geometric series. With weight 3 a, phi of N is 1 over 1 minus x cubed.

95
00:11:23,495 --> 00:11:28,039
With weight 2 b, phi of N is 1 over 1 minus x squared.

96
00:11:28,489 --> 00:11:35,187
By the Product Lemma, the answer is 1 over 1 minus x cubed, times 1 minus x squared.

97
00:11:35,637 --> 00:11:40,971
The product row has 2 at x to the 6. This matches the two orange cells.

98
00:11:41,421 --> 00:11:51,191
The Product Lemma extends to any finite number of factors, by induction. For k copies of A, the series is phi of A to the power k.

99
00:11:51,641 --> 00:12:04,719
Assignment 2, question 3 a uses this. Pause the video and try it. T n is the set of tuples of length n with entries 0, 1 or 2. The weight is the sum of the entries.

100
00:12:05,169 --> 00:12:15,729
Let A be the set 0, 1, 2, with weight u of a equal to a. Then T n is A to the n. The figure shows n equal to 3: three entries.

101
00:12:16,179 --> 00:12:24,541
Each entry contributes phi of A: 1 plus x plus x squared. There is one element of each weight 0, 1 and 2.

102
00:12:24,991 --> 00:12:35,061
The weight of a tuple is the sum of the weights of its entries. So by the Product Lemma, phi of T n is 1 plus x plus x squared, to the n.

103
00:12:35,511 --> 00:12:44,556
Check with n equal to 3. First, the square of 1 plus x plus x squared has the coefficients 1, 2, 3, 2, 1.

104
00:12:45,006 --> 00:12:57,529
Multiply by 1 plus x plus x squared again. Each new coefficient is the sum of three neighbours in the row above. For x cubed, that is 2 plus 3 plus 2, which is 7.

105
00:12:57,979 --> 00:13:02,224
So phi of T 3 is 1, 3, 6, 7, 6, 3, 1.

106
00:13:02,674 --> 00:13:08,498
Now count the tuples with sum 3. Six of them are the orders of 0, 1, 2.

107
00:13:08,948 --> 00:13:15,775
The seventh is 1, 1, 1. So there are 7 tuples, the same as the coefficient of x cubed.

108
00:13:16,225 --> 00:13:29,174
Part b. Pause the video and try it. An element of S n is a pair of disjoint subsets A and B of 1 to n. The weight is 2 times the size of A, plus the size of B.

109
00:13:29,624 --> 00:13:35,469
Draw a pair as a row of positions. Each position is in A, in B, or in neither.

110
00:13:35,919 --> 00:13:41,487
Here n is 3, A is 1, and B is 3. The weight is 2 plus 1, which is 3.

111
00:13:41,937 --> 00:13:47,249
Split S n by the size of A. Let S n k be the pairs with A of size k.

112
00:13:47,699 --> 00:13:51,198
These sets are disjoint, and their union is S n.

113
00:13:51,648 --> 00:13:59,456
Fix one set A of size k. Here A is 1, so k is 1. The set A gives x to the 2 k, here x squared.

114
00:13:59,906 --> 00:14:11,725
Each of the other n minus k positions can be in B or not. In B adds weight 1, and not in B adds 0. So each of these positions gives 1 plus x.

115
00:14:12,175 --> 00:14:21,732
So the sum over B is x to the 2 k, times 1 plus x to the n minus k. Here it is x squared times 1 plus x, squared.

116
00:14:22,182 --> 00:14:35,985
There are n choose k choices for A. So phi of S n k is n choose k, times x to the 2 k, times 1 plus x to the n minus k. The rows show k from 0 to 3.

117
00:14:36,435 --> 00:14:47,976
For example, k equal to 1 gives 3 times x squared, times 1 plus x squared. That is 3, 6, 3 at x squared, x cubed and x to the 4.

118
00:14:48,426 --> 00:14:52,991
By the Sum Lemma, phi of S n is the sum of these rows over k.

119
00:14:53,441 --> 00:15:01,847
For n equal to 3, the sum is 1, 3, 6, 7, 6, 3, 1. This is the same row as phi of T 3.

120
00:15:02,297 --> 00:15:09,379
Part c asks for a bijection from T n to S n that keeps the weight. Pause the video and try it.

121
00:15:09,829 --> 00:15:17,659
Send a tuple to the pair A, B. A holds the positions with entry 2. B holds the positions with entry 1.

122
00:15:18,109 --> 00:15:28,541
For example, the tuple 2, 1 has entry 2 at position 1, and entry 1 at position 2. So it goes to A equal to 1, and B equal to 2.

123
00:15:28,991 --> 00:15:35,732
The inverse g puts 2 at the positions in A, 1 at the positions in B, and 0 elsewhere.

124
00:15:36,182 --> 00:15:43,094
The figure shows all 9 elements of T 2 and their images. No two tuples have the same image.

125
00:15:43,544 --> 00:15:52,312
Now the weights. The weight of f of s is 2 times the number of entries equal to 2, plus the number of entries equal to 1.

126
00:15:52,762 --> 00:16:00,335
This is the sum of the entries, which is w of s. In the figure, w and v are equal on each arrow.

127
00:16:00,785 --> 00:16:05,009
A weight-preserving bijection gives equal generating series.

128
00:16:05,459 --> 00:16:18,579
Parts a and b then give the identity: 1 plus x plus x squared, to the n, equals the sum over k of n choose k, x to the 2 k, 1 plus x to the n minus k.

129
00:16:19,029 --> 00:16:24,448
Now strings. A k is the set of strings of length k, with letters from A.

130
00:16:24,898 --> 00:16:29,399
Here A has three letters. a and b have weight 1, and c has weight 2.

131
00:16:29,849 --> 00:16:33,775
A 0 has one element: the empty string, epsilon.

132
00:16:34,225 --> 00:16:43,462
A 1 holds the three letters. Each letter is a block, and the width of the block is its weight. So phi of A is 2 x plus x squared.

133
00:16:43,912 --> 00:16:47,496
The weight of a string is the sum of the weights of its letters.

134
00:16:47,946 --> 00:16:54,965
So the width of a string is its weight. For example, the string a c has weight 1 plus 2, which is 3.

135
00:16:55,415 --> 00:17:00,727
A 2 has 3 times 3, which is 9 strings: 3 choices for each letter.

136
00:17:01,177 --> 00:17:05,038
By the Product Lemma, phi of A k is phi of A to the k.

137
00:17:05,488 --> 00:17:11,355
Count A 2 by weight: 4 strings of weight 2, 4 of weight 3, and 1 of weight 4.

138
00:17:11,805 --> 00:17:19,805
This matches the square of 2 x plus x squared, which is 4 x squared plus 4 x cubed plus x to the 4.

139
00:17:20,255 --> 00:17:24,244
The union of all A k is A star, the set of all strings.

140
00:17:24,694 --> 00:17:33,419
The sets A k are disjoint, because each string has one length. The weight on A star is w k on the strings of length k.

141
00:17:33,869 --> 00:17:42,765
Lemma 2.13 says: w star is a weight function on A star if and only if no element of A has weight 0.

142
00:17:43,215 --> 00:17:47,226
First direction. Suppose z is in A, with weight 0.

143
00:17:47,676 --> 00:17:53,585
Then epsilon, z, z z, z z z, and so on are all strings of weight 0.

144
00:17:54,035 --> 00:18:02,249
There are infinitely many of them. So the set of strings of weight 0 is infinite, and w star is not a weight function.

145
00:18:02,699 --> 00:18:07,264
Second direction. Suppose every element of A has weight at least 1.

146
00:18:07,714 --> 00:18:13,815
Then a string of length k has weight at least k. So a string of weight n has length at most n.

147
00:18:14,265 --> 00:18:21,199
The gray zeros show this. For example, a string of length 3 cannot have weight 0, 1 or 2.

148
00:18:21,649 --> 00:18:32,187
For weight 3, only the lengths 0 to 3 are possible. Each A k has finitely many strings of weight 3, because w k is a weight function.

149
00:18:32,637 --> 00:18:38,568
A finite union of finite sets is finite. So w star is a weight function.

150
00:18:39,018 --> 00:18:47,167
Here is the String Lemma. If no element of A has weight 0, then phi of A star is 1 over 1 minus phi of A.

151
00:18:47,617 --> 00:18:53,761
The table uses the alphabet a, b, c from before. Row k is phi of A to the k.

152
00:18:54,211 --> 00:18:57,475
Row 0 is 1. It counts the empty string.

153
00:18:57,925 --> 00:19:01,147
Row 1 is phi of A: 2 x plus x squared.

154
00:19:01,597 --> 00:19:04,690
Rows 2 to 6 are the higher powers of phi of A.

155
00:19:05,140 --> 00:19:15,551
Now the proof. A star is the disjoint union of the sets A k. By the Infinite Sum Lemma, phi of A star is the sum of the series of the A k.

156
00:19:16,001 --> 00:19:19,563
By the Product Lemma, each term is phi of A to the k.

157
00:19:20,013 --> 00:19:30,893
Phi of A has no constant term, because no element has weight 0. So row k starts at x to the k. The purple staircase marks the forced zeros.

158
00:19:31,343 --> 00:19:43,695
So each column has only finitely many nonzero cells. For example, column x cubed has nonzero cells only in rows 2 and 3. So the sum is defined.

159
00:19:44,145 --> 00:19:50,908
The sum of all powers of phi of A is the geometric series: 1 over 1 minus phi of A.

160
00:19:51,358 --> 00:19:57,353
Add each column. For example, column x cubed gives 4 plus 8, which is 12.

161
00:19:57,803 --> 00:20:10,155
The column sums are 1, 2, 5, 12, 29, 70, 169. These are the coefficients of 1 over 1 minus 2 x minus x squared.

162
00:20:10,605 --> 00:20:20,098
A practice problem from Assignment 3. Pause the video and try it. The alphabet is a and b. a has weight 1, and b has weight 2.

163
00:20:20,548 --> 00:20:27,268
First, phi of A is x plus x squared. No letter has weight 0, so the String Lemma applies.

164
00:20:27,718 --> 00:20:33,201
By the String Lemma, phi of A star is 1 over 1 minus x minus x squared.

165
00:20:33,651 --> 00:20:49,693
To expand it, call the coefficients b n. Multiply both sides by 1 minus x minus x squared. This gives b n equals b n minus 1 plus b n minus 2, with b 0 and b 1 equal to 1.

166
00:20:50,143 --> 00:20:57,013
So the terms are 1, 1, 2, 3, 5. For example, b 4 is 3 plus 2, which is 5.

167
00:20:57,463 --> 00:21:01,836
Part c. The strings of weight 3 are a a a, a b, and b a.

168
00:21:02,286 --> 00:21:07,022
Each block covers its weight in units. So each string fills 3 units.

169
00:21:07,472 --> 00:21:11,440
There are 3 strings, and the coefficient of x cubed is 3.

170
00:21:11,890 --> 00:21:18,546
The drawing gives a check for any weight n: count the ways to fill n units with blocks of length 1 and 2.

171
00:21:18,996 --> 00:21:25,993
Part d changes the weights. Now a has weight 0, and b has weight 1. Pause the video and try it.

172
00:21:26,443 --> 00:21:31,329
The strings epsilon, a, a a, a a a, and so on all have weight 0.

173
00:21:31,779 --> 00:21:38,349
So infinitely many strings have weight 0. The coefficient of x to the 0 would be infinite.

174
00:21:38,799 --> 00:21:48,485
By Lemma 2.13, v star is not a weight function. The generating series does not exist, and the String Lemma does not apply.

175
00:21:48,935 --> 00:21:57,489
Practice problem 2. Pause the video and try it. B is the integers from 2 up. The weight of k is 2 k plus 1.

176
00:21:57,939 --> 00:22:06,110
So the weights are 5, 7, 9, 11, and so on. The smallest weight is 5, so no element has weight 0.

177
00:22:06,560 --> 00:22:12,341
Phi of B is x to the 5 plus x to the 7 plus x to the 9, and so on.

178
00:22:12,791 --> 00:22:22,903
Factor out x to the 5. The rest is a geometric series in x squared. So phi of B is x to the 5 over 1 minus x squared.

179
00:22:23,353 --> 00:22:28,303
By the String Lemma, phi of B star is 1 over 1 minus phi of B.

180
00:22:28,753 --> 00:22:39,633
Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x squared. The answer is 1 minus x squared, over 1 minus x squared minus x to the 5.

181
00:22:40,083 --> 00:22:43,027
This row is the expansion of phi of B star.

182
00:22:43,477 --> 00:22:47,381
Check one coefficient. Weight 12 has coefficient 2.

183
00:22:47,831 --> 00:22:52,481
The strings are 2, 3 and 3, 2, because 5 plus 7 is 12.

184
00:22:52,931 --> 00:22:57,305
A single element has an odd weight, so it cannot have weight 12.

185
00:22:57,755 --> 00:23:08,507
Practice problem 3. Pause the video and try it. A multiset with types 1 to t is a multiplicity vector. m i is the number of copies of i.

186
00:23:08,957 --> 00:23:13,906
The multiset 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3 has the vector 3, 1, 2.

187
00:23:14,356 --> 00:23:21,673
Its weight is the sum of its elements: 1 plus 1 plus 1 plus 2 plus 3 plus 3, which is 11.

188
00:23:22,123 --> 00:23:34,305
Each copy of i adds i. So the weight is the sum of i times m i: here 1 times 3, plus 2 times 1, plus 3 times 2. This is the posted answer.

189
00:23:34,755 --> 00:23:42,797
One step further, with the Product Lemma. M of t is N to the t. Coordinate i contributes i times m i.

190
00:23:43,247 --> 00:23:49,327
Its series is the sum of x to the i m, which is 1 over 1 minus x to the i.

191
00:23:49,777 --> 00:23:55,175
The rows show t equal to 3. Row i has a 1 at every multiple of i.

192
00:23:55,625 --> 00:24:02,921
By the Product Lemma, phi of M of t is the product over i of 1 over 1 minus x to the i.

193
00:24:03,371 --> 00:24:08,299
For t equal to 3, the product row starts 1, 1, 2, 3, 4.

194
00:24:08,749 --> 00:24:17,389
Check weight 4. The four vectors are 4, 0, 0; 2, 1, 0; 0, 2, 0; and 1, 0, 1.

195
00:24:17,839 --> 00:24:22,980
These are the multisets 1 1 1 1, 1 1 2, 2 2, and 1 3.

196
00:24:23,430 --> 00:24:27,270
A full answer has five parts. First, define the set.

197
00:24:27,720 --> 00:24:29,725
Second, define the weight function.

198
00:24:30,175 --> 00:24:36,447
Third, decompose the set: a disjoint union, a Cartesian product, or a set of strings.

199
00:24:36,897 --> 00:24:45,345
Show that the weight of the big set matches. For a product, the weight must be the sum of the weights of the parts, as in this example.

200
00:24:45,795 --> 00:24:51,278
Fourth, name the lemma: by the Sum Lemma, by the Product Lemma, or by the String Lemma.

201
00:24:51,728 --> 00:24:53,307
Fifth, write the series.

202
00:24:53,757 --> 00:25:01,586
For the String Lemma, also say why no element has weight 0. For the Sum Lemma, say why the parts are disjoint.

203
00:25:02,036 --> 00:25:08,159
To recap. For disjoint sets, the series add. This holds for infinitely many sets too.

204
00:25:08,609 --> 00:25:15,307
For a Cartesian product, with the weight of a pair equal to the sum of the weights, the series multiply.

205
00:25:15,757 --> 00:25:20,920
The set of strings has a weight function if and only if no letter has weight 0.

206
00:25:21,370 --> 00:25:25,146
Then the String Lemma gives 1 over 1 minus phi of A.

207
00:25:25,596 --> 00:25:32,209
In a written answer, define the set and the weight, decompose the set, name the lemma, and give the series.

208
00:25:32,659 --> 00:25:38,035
The next video uses the String Lemma for compositions: the set C is P star.
